Abstract

The built environment accounts for nearly 40% of global energy‑related carbon emissions, driven largely by heating, cooling, and the embodied carbon of conventional insulation materials. ThermSkin Materials addresses this challenge with a Hybrid Thermo‑Adaptive Bio‑Composite (HTBC) – a single‑panel, carbon‑negative building envelope that passively regulates indoor temperatures while meeting structural and hygric performance requirements. The core innovation is a 3‑in‑1 synergistic matrix: a bio‑based hemp‑lime structural skeleton, microencapsulated Phase Change Materials (PCM) for diurnal latent heat buffering, and silica aerogel nano‑insulation for ultra‑low thermal conductivity.

This Blueprint consolidates the Phase 1 technical architecture, a complete set of standard operating procedures (SOPs) for material qualification and performance validation, a supplier qualification framework, and a go‑to‑market strategy targeting the European and North American green construction sectors. Lab targets include λ ≤ 0.038 W/m·K (stretch goal 0.035 W/m·K), compressive strength ≥ 2.0 MPa, latent heat ≥ 120 kJ/kg, and embodied carbon ≤ ‑0.8 kg CO₂e/m². The 3‑year milestone is pilot production of 5 000 m²/yr with full EPD and CE certification.

The document is structured as a self‑contained Phase 1 investment and execution package, from scientific rationale to detailed lab protocols and commercial roll‑out.

