Chapter 2. Technical Architecture & Target Specifications

2.1 Material System: The 3‑in‑1 Matrix

The HTBC panel is not a random mixture but a functionally graded composite. Each component performs a distinct role, and their spatial arrangement is critical to long‑term performance.

Component Mass Fraction (Baseline) Role Key Property
Hemp hurd (woody core) 60% Structural filler, carbon sink, vapour‑open skeleton Biogenic carbon: ~1.7 kg CO₂ stored per kg hemp
Hydrated lime (Ca(OH)₂) + pozzolan 25% Binder, carbonation medium, pH buffer Carbonates to CaCO₃ over time, capturing atmospheric CO₂
Microencapsulated PCM 8% Latent heat thermal storage Melting point 22–26°C, enthalpy ≥120 J/g (core basis)
Silica aerogel granules 2% Nano‑insulation, thermal bridge suppression λ ≤ 0.022 W/m·K, hydrophobic
Water (process) ~10% (removed) Workability, hydration —

Critical Design Architecture:
Direct simultaneous mixing of all components causes three problems: (i) lime’s alkaline water attacks PCM polymer shells; (ii) aerogel granules clump, losing their insulating effect; (iii) PCM and aerogel compete for moisture during curing, reducing carbonation efficiency. The solution is a layered panel:

[Exterior lime render (5 mm)] → [PCM‑infused hemp‑lime core (50 mm)] → [Aerogel‑doped interface layer (5 mm)] → [Structural hemp‑lime backing (30 mm)]

This arrangement:

· Protects PCM microcapsules from external weather and internal alkaline migration.
· Concentrates aerogel where it most effectively blocks the primary heat flow path.
· Maintains a continuous vapour‑open hemp‑lime matrix through the panel thickness for moisture buffering.

