6.2 Environmental Product Declaration (EPD) Roadmap

An EPD (Type III, ISO 14025 / EN 15804+A2) is essential for green building certification and specification by architects. The LCA scope is cradle-to-gate with options (modules A1–A3, C1–C4, D).

LCA Module Inclusion Data Source
A1: Raw material supply Hemp cultivation, lime production, PCM synthesis, aerogel manufacture Supplier‑specific data or certified generic data (ecoinvent v3.10)
A2: Transport Transport of all raw materials to factory Actual logistics data from qualified suppliers
A3: Manufacturing Mixing, casting, CO₂ curing, finishing energy and consumables Pilot plant metered data; CO₂ uptake included as negative emission
C1–C4: End of life Deconstruction, transport, waste processing, disposal Conservative scenario: landfill for aerogel/PCM fraction; composting for hemp‑lime fraction
D: Benefits beyond system boundary Net CO₂ sequestration, avoided HVAC operation Modelling of operational energy reduction (30–40%) converted to CO₂ credits

Carbon Accounting (Biogenic + Carbonation):
The negative embodied carbon arises from two mechanisms:

1. Biogenic carbon storage: Hemp sequesters ~1.7 kg CO₂ per kg of dry hurd during growth. At 60% hemp content, a 1 m² panel (30 mm thick, ~15 kg dry mass) stores ~15.3 kg CO₂.
2. Lime carbonation: Ca(OH)₂ + CO₂ → CaCO₃ + H₂O. In a 14‑day accelerated cure, ~85% carbonation is achieved, capturing ~0.43 kg CO₂ per kg of lime. For 25% lime binder (~3.75 kg/m²), this removes ~1.6 kg CO₂/m².

Net balance: biogenic storage + carbonation minus process emissions from lime production (~0.78 kg CO₂/kg lime) and PCM/aerogel supply. The target cradle‑to‑gate result is ≤ –0.8 kg CO₂e/m², verified by a third‑party LCA practitioner.

Carbon Credit Monetisation:
Registration under Verra (VCS) or Puro.earth (Bio‑based Building Materials methodology) allows the negative emission to be sold as verified carbon removal units. At €80/tonne CO₂e and –0.8 kg/m², a 5,000 m² pilot yields 4 tonnes CO₂e removal per year, generating ~€320/yr initially, scaling to €40,000/yr at Year 3 volume.

6.3 REACH and Indoor Air Quality

· All components (lime, hemp, PCM, aerogel) must have REACH registrations for the EU market. Microencapsulated PCM shells (PMMA, melamine) are already registered.
· VOC and formaldehyde emissions testing (EN 16516) will be conducted in Phase 3 to support “low‑emitting materials” credits (LEED v5 EQ credit).
· The panel is free of halogenated flame retardants; fire class target is B‑s1,d0 or better (EN 13501-1). If necessary, non‑toxic mineral flame retardants (e.g., aluminium hydroxide) can be incorporated into the lime render without compromising breathability.

