Chapter 10. Supplier Qualification Strategy

10.1 Procurement Philosophy

The HTBC panel relies on four specialised raw materials, each with distinct supply chains, technical specifications, and sustainability credentials. A rigorous, standardised supplier qualification process ensures batch‑to‑batch consistency, regulatory compliance, and alignment with ThermSkin’s carbon‑negative mission. The full checklists for each material are provided in Chapter 12; this chapter summarises the evaluation framework and integration with the R&D timeline.

10.2 Qualification Process

1. Supplier Identification & RFQ: Issue a Request for Quotation package containing the material specifications (SOP‑01) and this qualification checklist. Target 3–5 candidates per material.
2. Paper Evaluation: Review mandatory criteria (certifications, technical data sheets, REACH status). Any failure → disqualification unless supplier provides a corrective action plan with fixed timeline.
3. Sample Testing: Request 1–5 kg sample from shortlisted suppliers. Execute full incoming material qualification per SOP‑01, including DSC for PCM, sieve analysis for hemp, titration for lime, and hydrophobicity/float test for aerogel.
4. Score and Rank: Apply weighted scoring (technical 50%, commercial 30%, sustainability 20%). Minimum approval threshold is 70/100.
5. Contract & Onboarding: Negotiate volume pricing, lead times, and quality assurance clauses. Enter approved suppliers into the supplier registry (Chapter 8).

10.3 Critical Material‑Specific Gate Criteria

Hemp Hurd:

· Origin: Cannabis sativa L., industrial varieties only.
· Particle size distribution: 2–10 mm, fines <5%.
· Moisture content: ≤12% on delivery.
· Organic or pesticide‑free cultivation strongly preferred.
· Biogenic carbon content documentation required for EPD.
· Instant fail: Mould, insect infestation, or chemical residue.

Hydrated Lime:

· Purity: Ca(OH)₂ ≥ 93%, MgO < 2%.
· Particle size: D50 < 15 µm; residue on 90 µm sieve ≤ 5%.
· Shelf life: ≥ 6 months unopened.
· Packaging: Waterproof, multi‑wall paper or PE‑lined bags.
· Instant fail: Pre‑carbonated (hard lumps, CO₂ content >3% by LOI) or unacceptably slow reactivity.

Microencapsulated PCM:

· Type: Bio‑based (capric/lauric eutectic) or paraffin C18, non‑halogenated.
· Melting point: 22–26°C (peak), onset ≥20°C.
· Latent heat: ≥ 120 J/g (core) or ≥ 95 J/g (encapsulated).
· Shell stability: No leakage after 24 h at 40°C on filter paper, and after mixing test at pH 12.5.
· Cycling stability: ΔH loss ≤ 5% after 100 cycles (supplier data) or ≤ 10% after 50 cycles (in‑house).
· Instant fail: Toxic shell composition, incompatible with alkaline environments, or REACH non‑compliance.

Silica Aerogel Granules:

· Type: Hydrophobic silica, granular form (not blanket or powder).
· Thermal conductivity: ≤ 0.022 W/m·K (granules).
· Particle size: D50 1.5–2.5 mm, span <1.5.
· Hydrophobicity: Float on water >30 min; contact angle >120°.
· Alkaline resistance: No degradation or hydrophobicity loss after 24 h in pH 12.5 solution.
· Instant fail: Dust content >1% fines, loss of float after alkaline exposure, or supplier unable to guarantee consistency.

10.4 Supplier Development and Risk Management

· Dual sourcing: For each material, maintain at least two approved suppliers, one primary and one qualified backup, to mitigate supply disruption.
· Forward contracts: Negotiate 12‑month price caps or volume commitments as soon as pilot demand projections are firm (Phase 2).
· Supplier audits: Conduct on‑site audits for the top two suppliers in each category by Month 9 to verify quality management systems.
· Sustainability alignment: Prefer suppliers with ISO 14001 certification, published carbon footprints, and a demonstrated commitment to reducing their own emissions. This aligns with ThermSkin’s Scope 3 reduction ambitions and EPD integrity.

10.5 Integration with R&D and Scale‑Up

Qualified suppliers are not merely vendors but potential development partners. Early engagement can unlock:

· Customised PCM shell formulations for alkaline environments.
· Tailored aerogel granule sizing or surface treatments.
· Co‑development of pre‑blended hemp‑lime dry mixes.

Supplier qualification is a continuous process; the checklist is reviewed quarterly to reflect new material specifications, emerging alternatives (e.g., bio‑char aerogel), and evolving regulatory requirements.



