📋 SOP-03: CO₂‑ACCELERATED CURING PROTOCOL

Objective
Achieve target mechanical strength and carbonation degree in 14 days (vs. 90 days ambient).

Chamber Setup

· Volume: 0.5–2 m³, sealed with gasketed door
· CO₂ supply: 99.5% purity, mass flow controller
· Sensors: CO₂ (NDIR), T, RH, O₂ (safety)
· Data logger: 5‑min interval

Procedure

1. Pre‑condition demolded samples: 24 h at 20°C/60% RH.
2. Load samples on perforated trays.
3. Set chamber parameters:
   · CO₂: 15±2%
   · Temperature: 25±1°C
   · RH: 70±5% (critical for carbonation kinetics)
4. Initiate curing cycle:
   · Days 1–3: Ramp CO₂ from 5% → 15% over 6 h
   · Days 4–14: Maintain steady state
   · Daily: 10‑min fresh air purge at 06:00
5. Monitor carbonation progress (weekly phenolphthalein spray test on sacrificial sample):
   · Pink = uncarbonated Ca(OH)₂; colorless = carbonated CaCO₃
   · Target: >85% depth carbonation by Day 14
6. Endpoint criteria (Day 14):
   · Compressive strength ≥ 1.8 MPa (pre‑screen)
   · Mass gain from CO₂ uptake ≥ 8% vs. initial dry mass
   · Surface pH ≤ 9.0 (vs. ~12.5 for fresh lime)
7. Post‑cure conditioning: 48 h at 20°C/50% RH before testing.

Troubleshooting

Symptom Probable Cause Corrective Action
Surface cracking RH too low (<60%) Increase humidification; reduce CO₂ ramp rate
Incomplete carbonation core Poor gas diffusion Increase tray spacing; add internal fans
Efflorescence Excess free lime + moisture Reduce initial water content; add pozzolan

Documentation: Form TS-CUR-003 (Curing Chamber Log); phenolphthalein test photos with scale bar.

---

📋 SOP-04: THERMAL CONDUCTIVITY (λ) TESTING — HFM METHOD

Objective
Measure steady‑state thermal conductivity per EN 12667.

Sample Preparation

· Dimensions: 300×300 mm, thickness 30±1 mm
· Surfaces: parallel within 0.1 mm; light sanding if needed
· Conditioning: 48 h at 23°C/50% RH

Equipment Setup

1. Calibrate HFM with NIST‑traceable reference materials (e.g., NIST SRM 1450d).
2. Set plate temperatures: hot 35°C, cold 15°C (ΔT = 20 K).
3. Apply thin layer of thermal grease; load sample at 5 kPa clamping pressure.

Test Procedure

4. Allow system to reach steady state (heat flux variation <0.5% over 30 min).
5. Record heat flux (q, W/m²), surface temperatures (T_hot, T_cold), sample thickness (L).
6. Calculate λ = (q × L) / (T_hot – T_cold).
7. Repeat for 3 orientations if anisotropy suspected.
8. Acceptance: λ ≤ 0.040 W/m·K (v1.0 baseline); CV ≤ 3% across n=3 samples.

Transient Hot‑Wire Screening (loose components)

· Probe insertion depth ≥10× particle size; test duration 10–30 sec; report λ at 25°C.

Documentation: Form TS-THM-004 (Thermal Conductivity Test Report); attach raw data, calibration certificate, sample placement photo.

---

📋 SOP-05: PCM CHARACTERIZATION — DSC & CYCLING STABILITY

Objective
Quantify latent heat capacity, transition temperature, and thermal cycling durability.

DSC Sample Prep

· Cryo‑mill cured HTBC to powder (<100 µm) under liquid N₂; weigh 10±0.1 mg into hermetically sealed aluminum pan.
· Option: Soxhlet extract PCM from 5 g HTBC with hexane to test recovered PCM as control.

DSC Protocol

1. Temperature program: Equilibrate 10°C → ramp 5°C/min to 40°C (melting) → hold 2 min → ramp 5°C/min to 10°C (crystallization) → hold 2 min → repeat 3 cycles.
2. N₂ purge 50 mL/min.
3. Data analysis: T_onset, T_peak, ΔH_melt, ΔH_cryst; subcooling ΔT = T_melt – T_cryst (target ≤3°C).
4. Acceptance: ΔH_melt (HTBC) ≥ 15 J/g; ΔH retention after 3 cycles ≥ 95%.

Accelerated Cycling Test (50 cycles)

5. Place 30×30×10 mm specimens in climate chamber.
6. Cycle profile: 18°C (4 h) → ramp 1°C/min → 28°C (4 h) → repeat; RH 50%.
7. Every 10 cycles: remove one specimen for DSC; inspect for leakage/cracking.
8. Endpoint (Cycle 50): ΔH loss ≤ 10%; no visible PCM exudation; mass loss ≤ 1%.

Documentation: Form TS-PCM-005 (DSC & Cycling Report); attach DSC curves, cycling chamber logs, pre/post‑cycling microscopy images.

---

📋 SOP-06: HYGROTHERMAL PERFORMANCE TESTING (CLIMATE CHAMBER)

Objective
Evaluate coupled heat and moisture transport per EN 15026.

Sample Configuration

· Panel: 300×300×30 mm; edges sealed with vapor‑impermeable tape
· Sensors (optional): T‑type thermocouples (3 depths), capacitive RH probes (2 depths)

Diurnal Swing Simulation

1. Exterior side: sinusoidal T cycle 10°C ↔ 30°C, period 24 h
2. Interior side: constant 22°C, 50% RH; air velocity 0.1 m/s
3. Duration: 7 days (3 days stabilization + 4 days data collection)
4. Measurements (5‑min interval): surface heat flux, interior surface T (IR camera spot), interior RH
5. Key indicators: Time lag τ (peak‑to‑peak delay), decrement factor f = (T_int,amp / T_ext,amp)
   · Target: τ ≥ 8 h; f ≤ 0.3

Moisture Buffering Test (ISO 24353)

6. After thermal test, switch to RH swing: 30% ↔ 70% (24 h cycle).
7. Measure mass change; calculate MBV_practical = Δm / (A × ΔRH).
8. Target: MBV ≥ 1.5 g/m²·%RH.

Documentation: Form TS-HYG-006 (Climate Chamber Test Report); time‑series plots, IR thermograms, model vs. experimental comparison.

---

📋 SOP-07: MECHANICAL & DURABILITY SCREENING

Compressive Strength (EN 1015-11)

1. Sample: 40×40×160 mm prisms; conditioning 48 h at 20°C/65% RH.
2. UTM: 50 kN load cell, crosshead 1 mm/min, platens parallel within 0.02 mm.
3. Test perpendicular to casting direction; record stress‑strain curve.
4. Calculate f_c = F_max / A_initial; modulus E = slope (0.1–0.4 f_c).
5. Acceptance: f_c ≥ 2.0 MPa (dry); ≥1.2 MPa after 5 freeze‑thaw cycles.

Freeze‑Thaw Resistance (EN 1015-21)

6. Saturation: 24 h water immersion.
7. Cycle: –15°C (4 h) ↔ +20°C water bath (4 h); 25 cycles.
8. Every 5 cycles: mass loss, visual damage, re‑test f_c on subset.
9. Acceptance: mass loss ≤ 3%; strength retention ≥ 80%.

Moisture Buffer Value (MBV) — Practical Method

10. Dry sample at 40°C to constant mass.
11. Expose to RH step 33% → 75% (saturated salt solutions).
12. Record mass gain every 30 min for 8 h.
13. Calculate MBV_practical = Δm / (A × ΔRH).
14. Acceptance: MBV ≥ 1.5 g/m²·%RH.

Documentation: Form TS-MEC-007 (Mechanical Test Report); stress‑strain curves, freeze‑thaw damage photos, MBV worksheet.

